api/b17-closed-period-write-path.test.js
  closing a pay period: who may, what is refused, and what is logged
  ok       W1. an OWNER closes a pay period: 200, the row is written with the period, and updated_by comes from the verified session
  ok       W2. the BILLING (office-manager/payroll) role closes a pay period: 200. Michael named owner PLUS the payroll person, so a suite that only drove owner would not have tested the decision
  ok       W3. a scheduler is REFUSED 403 and writes NO row
  ok       W3. a coordinator is REFUSED 403 and writes NO row
  ok       W3. a caregiver is REFUSED 403 and writes NO row
  ok       W5. ABLATION -- with MANAGEMENT_ROLES widened to include scheduler, a scheduler passes the OUTER management gate and is STILL refused 403 by SEN_PERIOD_CLOSE_ROLES. Without this arm the new gate cannot fire and is indistinguishable from dead code
  ok       W6. a write that DROPS an already-closed period is 409 CLOSED_PERIOD_IMMUTABLE, names the affected period, and writes nothing. Un-closing a period restates wages already paid -- the same defect the freeze exists to prevent, by a different door and with no trail at all
  ok       W7. CONTROL -- ADDING a period while keeping the existing one is 200 with both. Append-only is append-ONLY, not freeze-forever
  ok       W8. a period whose paid_at cannot be read is 400 INVALID_SETTING at the WRITE, naming paid_at, and nothing is stored. Stored, it would refuse every pay run from then on with the author long gone
  ok       W9. value.periods missing is 400, not an empty list -- an absent array read as "nothing is closed" would unfreeze every paid period
audit log write failed: pay_period_closed 400 violates check constraint "sairnsenior_audit_log_event_type_check"
  ok       W10. a FAILED audit write REFUSES the close (503 AUDIT_UNAVAILABLE) and nothing is stored. This is the OPPOSITE of auditPinSetup's best-effort posture and the trade is the other way: a grant blocked by a logging outage locks somebody out of their own app, while a pay-period close is retryable and is audited precisely because it freezes money
  ok       W11. the audit row names the event, the employee, the role AND the period(s) ADDED with their paid_at -- "settings changed" would not tell an auditor which wages got frozen
  ok       W12. a FAILED read of the existing list is 503 and writes nothing -- treating it as empty would let an already-closed period be dropped silently, which is W6 defeated by a transport blip
  ok       W13. the event_type this branch emits is INSIDE the audit table's CHECK constraint. An event_type missing from a CHECK is a silently dropped audit row everywhere else on this platform; here it is a refusal, so it would be loud -- but it would still block every close
  ok       W14. SEN_PERIOD_CLOSE_ROLES is { owner, billing } and is a SEPARATE object from MANAGEMENT_ROLES, declared with its own roleSet() call. Aliasing it would reintroduce exactly the silent widening W5 drives

